-- title:
--   Two scales whose edge-length windows share a value differ by a bounded amount
-- statement:
--   Let $\Delta, R, L \in \mathbb{R}$ with $\Delta \ge 2$ and $R > 0$, and let $C \in \mathbb{N}$ satisfy
--   $$2\Delta R \;\le\; 2^{C}.$$
--   Let $m_1, m_2 \in \mathbb{N}$ and suppose that the single number $L$ lies below the top of the window attached to $m_1$ and above the bottom of the window attached to $m_2$:
--   $$L < 2\Delta^{\,m_1+1}R \qquad\text{and}\qquad \Delta^{\,m_2} \le L .$$
--   Then
--   $$m_2 \;\le\; m_1 + C .$$
--
--   The two conditions say that the length $L$ is compatible with both scales, the edges produced at scale $m$ having lengths in the window $[\Delta^{m}, 2\Delta^{m+1}R)$. The conclusion is that such windows overlap only boundedly: a given edge length can be carried by at most $C+1$ scales above any fixed one, so a multiplicity count over scales contributes only the constant factor $2C+1$.
